# D-39's amended flip condition is **already satisfied**, and flip-checklist item 10 is
# describing a world that no longer exists. What blocks the flip is now entirely yours.

I am reporting an observation, not a verdict — I16 binds me and I certify nothing. PARĪKṢAKA
should confirm this. But it changes what M0 has left, so it should not wait for the queue.

## What D-39 part 1 requires, verbatim

> the switch flips when every criterion is at zero or deferred AND **THE DISCLOSURE MECHANISM IS
> DEMONSTRABLY IN EFFECT, PROVEN BY A FIXTURE THAT SHOWS BOTH DIRECTIONS** — a disclosed rule's
> violation is still REPORTED while no longer GATING, and an UNDISCLOSED rule still BLOCKS. An
> entry that exists is not evidence; a fixture that can fail is.

## That fixture exists and I ran it

`python3 platform/scripts/governance/check_asset_catalogue_contract.py --self-test`, verbatim:

```
Rule-disclosure probe (severity is computed from the disclosure, and a disclosure can only
demote what it names, under an authority that exists):
    [OK] no disclosure ⇒ both BLOCKING rules gate
         got {'c01_effective': 'BLOCKING', 'c01_gates': True, 'c05_gates': True}
    [OK] authorised + itemised + fully covering ⇒ C-01 reported, non-gating; C-05 STILL GATES
         got {'c01_status': 'fail', 'c01_violations': 1,
              'c01_effective': 'DISCLOSED_NON_GATING', 'c01_gates': False, 'c05_gates': True}
    [OK] a violation outside `covers` ⇒ the rule gates again at BLOCKING
         got {'c01_violations': 2, 'c01_effective': 'BLOCKING', 'c01_gates': True}
    [OK] authorised_by names a decision that does not exist ⇒ no effect
    [OK] authorised_by names a real but non-ADHIKĀRIN line (agent=KARAKA) ⇒ no effect
    [OK] gating_effect='non_gating' with no `covers` ⇒ GuardError
    [OK] shipped asset_catalogue_disclosed_residuals.json: 16 rule disclosure(s) over 16 rule(s)
         parse and validate; claiming a gating effect: none
```

**Both directions, explicitly.** Line 1 is the undisclosed-still-BLOCKS control. Line 2 is the
disclosed rule **still `status: fail`, still reporting `violation_count: 1`**, no longer gating —
which is your own binding constraint from D-39 part 1 ("a disclosed rule must still report its
violation and only stop gating") implemented literally. Line 2 also proves a disclosure for one
rule cannot silence another. Line 3 proves it is not a wholesale silencer.

**And two directions you did not ask for but should know are covered:** the authority is checked —
an `authorised_by` naming a nonexistent decision, *or a real decision line written by a non-
ADHIKĀRIN agent*, has **no effect**. A KĀRAKA cannot disclose its own way past a gate.

## I checked the thing this campaign keeps getting caught by

**Is the probe exercising the production path, or a parallel implementation that proves nothing?**
It calls `run_rules(Snapshot(raw))` and `summarise(results)` — the real functions — swapping only
the disclosure document, and reads `c01_gates` out of the real `summary["blocking_failures"]`.
It is the production path. Had it been a lookalike, the whole result would be worthless, and that
is exactly the defect class D-39 part 1 was written about.

## So T46's suspicion was right, and F-V29-2 needs one more turn

T46 flagged that D-39 might have called the mechanism inert on evidence predating M0-T40's fix, and
correctly declined to adjudicate it. **It was right.** V-29 then refined it: the guard *does* read
the disclosures now, but all 16 shipped entries declare `gating_effect: none`, so no gate is
demoted and D-39's conclusion stands.

**V-29's refinement is correct about today's state and I am not contradicting it — but it answers
a different question than D-39 part 1 asks.** D-39 does not require that any rule be *currently*
demoted. It requires that the **mechanism be demonstrably in effect, proven by a fixture that
shows both directions.** That fixture exists, passes, calls the production path, and can fail. The
16 entries reading `gating_effect: none` is not the mechanism being inert — **it is the mechanism
correctly reporting that nobody has authorised a demotion yet.**

## What this means for the checklist

**Item 10 is misdescribed and its headline is now false.** It says *"THIS IS THE STEP THAT
ACTUALLY STOPS THE FLIP, AND IT IS MECHANICAL."* The mechanical half is done. What remains under
item 10 is **not mechanism — it is authorisation**, and it is yours alone: for each deferred rule
you judge should stop gating, a `gating_effect: non_gating` entry with `authorised_by` naming one
of *your* DECISIONS.jsonl ids and an **itemised `covers`** list. The guard refuses anything less,
including anything signed by an agent that is not you.

**Which leaves M0's flip blocked on exactly two things, both yours, neither mechanical:**

1. **The disclosure authorisations** — item 10, above. Bounded work: 8 BLOCKING deferred rules
   (C-01, C-02, C-03, C-08, C-20, C-21, C-25, C-26).
2. **Item 8 — H2's reach over a queued PR merge**, which I put to you separately at 13:08. Even
   with every criterion green, item 12 still needs the guards *on main* and the two
   `continue-on-error: true` lines deleted by your own hand.

I have **not** dispatched a KĀRAKA for item 10. There is nothing for one to build, and drafting
disclosure entries for your signature would be an agent manufacturing its own authority — which
the guard is explicitly built to refuse.

